to communicate information

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:21.150
to different stakeholders.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.030
So as part of our
demonstration scenario,

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:25.400
we're showing different types
of tools that are targeted

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.000
at different types of users--

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.990
whether it's technical users,
or business stakeholders-- that

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:33.110
is actually commensurate
with the type of usage

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:35.780
that they will get from the
system, or the type of insights

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:36.614
they want to derive.

00:11:36.614 --> 00:11:39.113
So I'll be showing different
visualization tools, as well as

00:11:39.113 --> 00:11:41.000
collaboration tools
that integrate directly

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.220
into the platform.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:44.990
As we scale analytics,
it's important

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:48.170
that we don't ignore
data privacy concerns.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:49.640
So as part of our
scenario, we'll

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:51.500
be showing different
technologies that

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:55.610
allow you to factor
in private information

00:11:55.610 --> 00:12:00.570
and avoid having that exposed
in our analytics pipeline.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:03.230
So we'll be showing some
APIs that will accomplish

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:05.600
that capability at scale.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.670
And then, lastly,
with the combination

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.380
of all these different
elements, we'll

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:11.630
hopefully have a system
ready for Bill's Grocer that

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.600
will not just mean a
happy set of customers,

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:16.930
but ultimately, those
happy customers leading

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:18.590
to a happy business for Bill.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:20.480
So with that, I want
to pass back to William

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.332
to elaborate further.

00:12:22.332 --> 00:12:23.540
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE: Thank you.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:24.331
Can I take the box?

00:12:24.331 --> 00:12:25.490
Thank you.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.380
So when we talk
about the toolset,

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:29.999
maybe one way to set the
stage to help understand

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:31.790
how we came to the
stage where we are right

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:34.940
now is to think about the
history of data processing

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:37.430
at Google, which,
as you can imagine,

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.000
started long before
Google had a cloud.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.830
You know, for the first
part of its existence,

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.980
Google was only building
technology for its own use,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:48.290
starting research,
and other services.

00:12:48.290 --> 00:12:50.900
And so, when you start as
a company with the goal

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.210
to index the entire
web, obviously you

00:12:53.210 --> 00:12:55.280
have a scale problem
from day one.

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:59.090
But also, solving scale is
actually not the hardest part.

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.310
The hardest part is
solving scale efficiently,

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:04.340
doing it in a way that is very
cost efficient from a resource

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.550
perspective, also from a human
productivity perspective.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.760
And that's how Google can
offer services for free.

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:13.140
Its really dealing with
scale and productivity.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.780
And so there's a long list of
papers that were published.

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.520
Those are internal technology
that are now known to the world

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.950
because Google published papers.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.740
And at the time, Google
just published the papers

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.790
and then moved on, to develop
the next internal system.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:31.070
People outside Google,
reading those papers

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:34.280
and learning how
Google was using that,

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.827
wanted to have access to them.

00:13:35.827 --> 00:13:37.660
And at the time, the
only way to have access

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:38.830
was to reimplement it.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:42.260
And so systems like Hadoop,
and Edgebase, and others

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.890
were created, basically, by
reimplementing those papers

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:47.690
that had been published.

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:49.980
From a Google perspective,
that was fine.

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:51.650
That's why we
published the paper.

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:55.490
But we were not involved in
those non-Google use cases.

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:59.140
That changed with the advent
of Cloud in several ways.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:01.970
So what has not changed is
we still publish papers.

00:14:01.970 --> 00:14:05.300
So recently, DataFlow and
TensorFlow-- and there

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:08.730
are more coming up that
are in review stage.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:10.790
But now, when we
publish a paper,

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:13.670
we also open source
the code behind it.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.400
And so TensorFlow
is now open source.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:19.480
Apache Beam, which we'll
talk about a bit more later,

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:23.570
is the result of Google open
sourcing the DataFlow SDK,

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:25.580
giving it to the
Apache Foundation.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.830
And so, instead of
having to re-implement,

00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:30.230
people can now use
that using the code

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:32.240
that Google uses internally.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.350
And the services themselves are
made available on Google Cloud.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.840
And so we'll start with
BigQuery as a first example,

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.520
and then we'll talk
about some others.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.940
So BigQuery is kind
of the successor

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:46.040
of the original Dremel engine,
which of course has seen

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.860
a lot of improvements
and re-engineering

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:50.730
over the last 10 years.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.840
But it is one of the
workhorses of data processing

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:56.870
at Google, available
as a cloud service.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.650
So let's talk a bit
more about BigQuery.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:02.450
In a way, the
one-line description

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:04.610
is a cloud data warehouse.

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.710
And that's just-- that's
both good and bad.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:10.370
In a way, it's true, because
anything a data warehouse

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:11.750
can do, BigQuery can do.

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.690
You use standard SQL
to interact with it.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:18.470
You can use all your favorite
data visualization tools.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:22.740
You know, it has ODBC, JDBC
drivers, an API, a CLI,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.700
a rich ecosystem of partners.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:28.220
You know, it is a true,
first-class enterprise data

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.210
warehouse.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:30.920
It's also more than
that in the way

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:32.700
that it's a cloud-native
data warehouse.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:36.140
And Reza, in his demo, will
call out some of the aspects.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.520
It's serverless.

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:40.910
It's separate storage
from processing.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:43.290
And, as we announced
this morning,

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:46.580
it also has a lot
of new capabilities

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:47.990
that make it much
more versatile.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:51.020
And so this morning, we
announced BigQuery ML

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:54.590
to do machine learning
directly in BigQuery using SQL.

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.380
We also announced an
upcoming feature--

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.840
to have GIS queries.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:05.870
So you'll have geo-queries
based on location information

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:11.010
in your data that allows you
to query in a geo-specific way.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:14.180
And so that's another example
of the versatility of BigQuery

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:17.090
that is pretty
cool to the system.

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:20.330
One aspect is, how do you
get data into BigQuery?

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.989
You know, because, to be
useful, it has to have data.

00:16:22.989 --> 00:16:24.530
Now, that being
said, there's already

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.710
a lot of public datasets
that are available directly

00:16:27.710 --> 00:16:28.640
in BigQuery.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:32.150
And so you can go and try
and use it at large scale

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:33.830
without bringing your own data.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:36.710
But most customers, obviously,
want to bring their own data.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:38.770
Many ways to do that.

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:41.660
A lot of marketing data can
be brought automatically,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.650
especially if it's
already at Google.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:46.420
You know, if it's ad
data, or analytics data,

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:48.900
or if you're a YouTube
publisher, all of that

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.060
data, you can just
say, I want that data

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:52.227
brought into BigQuery.

00:16:52.227 --> 00:16:54.060
You have nothing to do
there other than just

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.050
saying, "I want it."

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:59.280
We have a rich ecosystem
of partners in the ETL data

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:04.030
integration space to bring
data from enterprise software

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:08.430
on-prem, from other
clouds, and SaaS vendors.

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.880
And then, on the
right side, if you

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.420
build systems that
write to BigQuery,

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:17.579
BigQuery is very well-equipped
to do streaming ingestion.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:20.880
And so events, flying through
Pub/Sub or other streaming

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.640
system, you can have
them flow into BigQuery

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.190
and be immediately
available in your queries.

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:28.890
You know, you just pump
events and query on them,

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:32.400
so very easy, very optimized for
streaming ingestion, as well,

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:35.610
of course, as supporting
batch ingestion through GCS--

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.850
Google Cloud Storage--
including taking advantage

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:41.790
of the many ways we
can bring data to GCS,

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:46.920
including cloud storage transfer
service to bring data online

00:17:46.920 --> 00:17:47.975
or offline.

00:17:47.975 --> 00:17:49.350
You know, you can
bring petabytes

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.810
using physical
devices, and many ways

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:55.680
to bring data to GCS that
can flow then into BigQuery.

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.690
And so let's see
that in practice,

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.765
and Reza will show us.

00:17:59.765 --> 00:18:01.040
REZA ROKNI: Thank you.

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:03.649
So let me just switch this over.

00:18:03.649 --> 00:18:04.791
Great.

00:18:04.791 --> 00:18:05.290
OK.

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.560
So what we're going to
do is run some queries

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.120
against a couple of datasets.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:13.640
Now, Win mentioned
click stream data--

00:18:13.640 --> 00:18:15.700
so this is the data
coming from the website.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.830
So we're going to run
a query against data

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:20.380
that we've obtained by Google
Analytics 360 Premium, that

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:21.910
has been loaded into BigQuery.

00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:23.590
We're also going
to run some queries

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:27.204
against a transactional
point-of-sale dataset.

00:18:27.204 --> 00:18:28.870
Now, this is actually
a petabyte of data

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:30.760
that we're going to run
some queries against,

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.680
just to show the
ability of BigQuery

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:36.250
to run queries against
very large datasets.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:39.100
Now, before we start, I'd like
to point out one of the pieces

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:43.150
that William brought up around
BigQuery's architecture.

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:46.540
So BigQuery separates
processing from storage--

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:49.200
which is part of the reason it
is able to do what it can do.

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:50.491
But what does that mean to you?

00:18:50.491 --> 00:18:52.870
So the first thing
it provides is

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:55.390
that, if you're not running
any queries on BigQuery,

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.210
you don't need to pay for
any servers that are sitting

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.200
around waiting for work.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:01.570
So that's one aspect.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:04.870
The other aspect is around
the sharing of data,

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:08.950
both within the organization
and from external sources

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:10.370
to the organization.

00:19:10.370 --> 00:19:11.290
So how does that work?

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:12.998
If we think about a
traditional database,

00:19:12.998 --> 00:19:15.490
you have your software that
you install into some machines,

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:17.827
then you attach SAN,
NAS, disk, et cetera.

00:19:17.827 --> 00:19:18.910
This thing becomes a unit.

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:21.280
You then push
information into it.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.590
And now, to get access
to that information,

00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:26.290
you either need to give access
directly to the database--

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:27.580
I used to be a DBA.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.850
This is not something I would
let people do very easily--

00:19:30.850 --> 00:19:33.262
or you have an
application layer that you

00:19:33.262 --> 00:19:34.970
need to build on top
and deal with things

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:38.110
like multi-tenancy, et cetera,
or you do the FTP dance.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.270
And nobody likes to
do the FTP dance.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:43.640
It's tedious and boring and
leads to lots of problems.

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:45.250
So what we're going
to do is actually

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.125
show the separation of
processing and storage

00:19:47.125 --> 00:19:48.250
and what it means.

00:19:48.250 --> 00:19:51.530
Here we have the new
BigQuery user interface.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:53.800
It was recently pushed
out with this new version,

00:19:53.800 --> 00:19:56.530
for those of you who've
used BigQuery before.

00:19:56.530 --> 00:20:00.285
On the top here, we can see
that I am in the DA101 project.

00:20:00.285 --> 00:20:01.660
So this is the
project I am going

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:03.700
to be running my queries from.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.510
On the left-hand side, as
well as the DA101 project,

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:09.340
we can actually
see other projects

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.860
that I have been
granted access to.

00:20:11.860 --> 00:20:14.600
So through the proper
security and authorization ACL

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:17.500
mechanisms, I've been given
access to these datasets.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:20.910
Now, this particular dataset,
the retail petabyte dataset,

00:20:20.910 --> 00:20:24.250
which I'm going to highlight
here, has some tables in it.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:27.910
And again, this is a table that
exists in a different project.

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:29.330
I've just been
given access to it,

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:31.420
so I'll run my query
from here against that.

00:20:31.420 --> 00:20:33.180
This sales partition table--

00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:36.830
just to look at the structure--
it's got a sales order key.

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:40.010
This is that
point-of-sale information.

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:41.800
And if we look at
the types of data

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.510
that we're allowed
to store, as well

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:46.510
as the standard ones you have
here, we have a record type.

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:49.194
So this record, there's
a single sales order key,

00:20:49.194 --> 00:20:50.860
and there are many
lines of information,

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.080
because BigQuery
supports repeated fields

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:54.970
within a column.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:58.750
So let's go and actually
look at some more details

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.182
around this table.

00:21:00.182 --> 00:21:02.140
So here, we're going to
start discovering that,

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:06.460
despite the name, Bill's Grocer
is actually hugely successful,

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:10.180
because they have over one
petabyte of data in their sales

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.580
information-- which we
can highlight here--

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:16.254
and over one trillion
rows of information.

00:21:16.254 --> 00:21:18.670
So that's a lot of sales that
these guys have been making.

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:20.909
I'm assuming it's a
very big Bill's Grocer.

00:21:20.909 --> 00:21:22.450
Now, what we're
going to do is, we're

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.340
going to run a deliberately
inefficient query.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:27.890
So this particular query
that I'm going to run,

00:21:27.890 --> 00:21:31.090
I'm going to try and find
the sales information to date

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:34.620
for a particular customer,
denoted by this customer key.

00:21:34.620 --> 00:21:36.370
I'm going to kick that
off now, and that's

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.078
going to take a little
while to complete.

00:21:38.078 --> 00:21:41.170
Just a note-- actually,
that was cheating.

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:44.680
Let me turn off caching.

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:47.900
So BigQuery will cache results
of a query that's been recently

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.404
run, for up to 24 hours.

00:21:49.404 --> 00:21:50.320
Let me run that again.

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:52.611
So that's now going to take
a little while to complete.

00:21:52.611 --> 00:21:55.360
What I'd like to point out
on the right-hand side is,

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:58.720
even though there's a petabyte
of information in that table,

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:00.490
we're only going to
process 31 terabytes.

00:22:00.490 --> 00:22:02.240
It's still a pretty big number.

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:04.281
And we're still going to
process all of the rows.

00:22:04.281 --> 00:22:06.700
The reason for that is BigQuery
has a column I/O storage

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:07.480
structure.

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:10.300
So what that means is, if
you're going to only access

00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:12.250
a few of the columns
from the table

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.140
within your select
statement, it only

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.090
needs to process
those columns, and it

00:22:16.090 --> 00:22:17.890
ignores the rest of the data.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:21.250
So here, it doesn't need
to access any other columns

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:23.500
other than the three
we have in this query.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:25.833
Now, because that's going to
take a little while to run,

00:22:25.833 --> 00:22:28.420
I'm going to quickly
move over to another tab

00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:31.180
and show some of the other
features of BigQuery.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:33.610
So first of all, what
I'd like to point out

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.832
is this is the area that
we can actually start

00:22:35.832 --> 00:22:37.040
building our queries against.

00:22:37.040 --> 00:22:43.270
So we can start typing in
and we can do autocomplete.

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:45.580
However, when we're doing
ad hoc queries, et cetera,

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.380
it's obviously nice
to have a scratch pad

00:22:47.380 --> 00:22:50.560
area, so somewhere where you'll
save your queries for use

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:52.040
by yourself later on.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:54.220
So for that, if we
look at Saved Queries,

00:22:54.220 --> 00:22:55.450
we have two environments.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:57.370
We have the Personal
Queries, which

00:22:57.370 --> 00:23:00.370
is my personal area
where I can leave queries

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:01.900
that I want to come back to.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:03.960
Or, if you want to
share your queries--

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.250
so you've built that beautiful
query, 30 lines long,

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:08.749
and you want to share it with
your colleagues and show off--

00:23:08.749 --> 00:23:10.940
we can actually do that
through Project Queries.

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:12.761
So here, we can save
our queries and then

00:23:12.761 --> 00:23:15.010
share it with my colleagues,
like William and Win, who

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:18.730
can then go and access this
query, work on it, or run it.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:21.700
And we're going to choose a
particular query, which is

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:25.420
this Google Analytics 360 data.

00:23:25.420 --> 00:23:28.810
This particular query is going
to look at the click stream

00:23:28.810 --> 00:23:31.570
data that's been imported
from Google Analytics 360,

00:23:31.570 --> 00:23:36.820
and it's going to tell me which
sources have generated the most

00:23:36.820 --> 00:23:40.400
bounce rates from our dataset.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:43.720
Now, by "bounce rate," we mean
a user has just come and not

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:46.300
viewed any more pages on
the first page they came to.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:51.120
So first of all, I'm going
to load this query in.

00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:53.790
And see, it's been
loaded into the top here.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:56.310
I'm going to run this
query now, and that

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:59.430
will take about five,
six seconds to complete.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:00.120
It's finished.

00:24:00.120 --> 00:24:03.000
And we can see here that
it's gone through the Google

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.670
Analytics 360 data,
and provided insights

00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:11.097
to us about the sources that
have been sending clicks to us,

00:24:11.097 --> 00:24:12.180
and the number of visits--

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:16.140
the number of times that we've
only landed on a single page.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.570
Just to show some of the
other features of BigQuery--

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:22.140
so on this side,
I'm going to create

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.170
a dataset, which is
the container that we

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:26.850
can load tables into.

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:29.130
Here we have the Dataset ID.

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:32.670
But on data locations, we
can choose several regions--

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:35.597
United States, European
Union, or Tokyo.

00:24:35.597 --> 00:24:37.680
So these are the regions
that the data will reside

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.230
in when we create this dataset.

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:42.630
In terms of the
other functions here,

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:45.180
we have a default
table expiration.

00:24:45.180 --> 00:24:46.230
So this is super useful.

00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:48.270
When you're actually
building out some queries,

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:50.820
sometimes you want to build
some temporary tables.

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:53.251
There's a couple of reasons
you'll want to do that.

00:24:53.251 --> 00:24:55.500
For example, you're running
a particularly large query

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:57.291
and you want to explore
the dataset that is

00:24:57.291 --> 00:24:58.950
the result of that large query.

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:02.520
So for that, you'll just run the
large query, create this table,

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.320
and you don't want
to then have to think

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:06.130
about deleting it afterwards.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:09.270
So by creating a dataset
with this expiration level,

00:25:09.270 --> 00:25:10.890
we can say number of days.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.990
Let's say we create a
dataset called "temporary."

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:14.489
We can create these
temporary tables

00:25:14.489 --> 00:25:16.260
and know that, after
about five, six days,

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:18.570
they're automatically going
to get cleaned up for us.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:20.910
The other reason you also
need temporary tables

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:24.687
is when you are building a
particularly large query,

00:25:24.687 --> 00:25:26.270
and it's very useful
sometimes to just

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:27.811
take out the inner
part of that query

00:25:27.811 --> 00:25:31.330
while you explore
the outer parts.

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:32.490
I'm going to now--

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:34.950
also, I said that
the query that we

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.520
were running against that very
large dataset was inefficient.

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.100
Just to show a more efficient
version of that query using

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:44.910
some features that recently
released around clusters

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:48.430
and a slightly older feature
around partition tables--

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:53.650
so if I go to Saved Queries,
and look at our Project Queries,

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:58.840
and customer cluster optimized--
so I'm going to open this one.

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:03.220
So this particular query
is making use of some dates

00:26:03.220 --> 00:26:07.600
to limit the pool, and
it's using a specific range

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:08.740
of customer keys.

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:11.680
If we look at this sales
partitioned cluster table,

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:14.230
it is of a similar size to
the table we saw before--

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:15.810
around a petabyte.

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.770
But when I run this
query, it's actually

00:26:17.770 --> 00:26:20.560
going to complete in about--

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:24.810
again, I need to turn this off.

00:26:28.618 --> 00:26:29.570
There we go.

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:31.760
So this will complete in
about 10 to 20 seconds.

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:33.343
And if we look on
the right-hand side,

00:26:33.343 --> 00:26:35.343
it's actually having to
process a lot less data.

00:26:35.343 --> 00:26:37.009
And the reason for
that is it's actually

00:26:37.009 --> 00:26:38.290
using some smarts around--

00:26:38.290 --> 00:26:40.460
because the partition
table is by date,

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:42.100
it's been able to
prune information

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:43.300
it doesn't need to access.

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:46.914
And it's clustered
by our customer key.

00:26:46.914 --> 00:26:48.580
So that is going to
complete, hopefully,

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:50.660
in a few more seconds.

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:51.170
There we go.

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:52.961
That was the result of
that one finishing--

00:26:52.961 --> 00:26:53.830
18 seconds.

00:26:53.830 --> 00:26:56.770
And now we're going to go
back to the original query.

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:59.500
And here, we see that that
larger query processed

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:01.930
31 terabytes, went
through a trillion rows

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:06.130
in two minutes and just a
few seconds on top of that.

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.350
Now, the last thing I
want to show you here

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:10.330
is this table stock.

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:14.180
If I go to this stock
table and look at details--

00:27:14.180 --> 00:27:15.810
we expand this further up here.

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.590
And I'm not able to
use the scroll bar.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:25.380
There we go-- we notice that
it's actually got streaming

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:27.570
buffer stats underneath.

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:30.000
One of the things
that William mentioned

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:33.210
is that BigQuery supports
streaming inserts directly

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:36.480
into BigQuery, which means we
can continuously update tables,

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:39.400
and when we make a query call,
it's using the latest data.

00:27:39.400 --> 00:27:40.980
So here, the
point-of-sale system

00:27:40.980 --> 00:27:44.382
is continually updating
sales to BigQuery.

00:27:44.382 --> 00:27:45.840
And when we run a
query, it's going

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:47.700
to be with the most
up-to-date data.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:52.330
Now, if we can go
back to the slides--

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:52.830
OK.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:55.490
And now the ticker.

00:27:55.490 --> 00:27:58.070
So how do we get that streaming?

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.160
So one of the ways
is through the API

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:01.480
that BigQuery directly exposes.

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.030
And you can build applications
that use that API directly.

00:28:05.030 --> 00:28:07.180
However, most of the
customers I work with

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:09.250
will actually use
other mechanisms

00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:11.680
to be able to do this,
predominantly using things

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:12.880
like Pub/Sub and Dataflow.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:14.920
So if we think about
a streaming pipeline,

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:17.020
when we've got
messages coming in,

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:19.810
we need some way of actually
absorbing those messages.

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:22.150
And Google Cloud Pub/Sub
is our global publish

00:28:22.150 --> 00:28:23.980
and subscribe API.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:26.500
It's able to absorb
messages onto a topic.

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:29.120
And it also acts as a
very good shock absorber.

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:32.910
So if you imagine you have a
very high spike in requests

00:28:32.910 --> 00:28:35.320
that you weren't expecting,
or you have some problems

00:28:35.320 --> 00:28:37.600
downstream, Pub/Sub
will retain information

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:38.560
for up to seven days.

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:40.900
And I've seen it used very
effectively in that mode

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:43.059
with quite a few customers.

00:28:43.059 --> 00:28:45.100
So now that we've got a
way of actually absorbing

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:48.220
these messages, we need a way of
transporting them from Pub/Sub

00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:50.680
over to the BigQuery table.

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.540
And for that, we're
going to use DataFlow,

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:54.610
which allows you to
do batch and stream

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.500
processing in a distributed
way, being able to scale out

00:28:57.500 --> 00:28:59.797
at very large scale.

00:28:59.797 --> 00:29:01.630
If we think of the
end-to-end pipeline here,

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:03.340
we have the
point-of-sale system.

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:05.140
There is an application
that is writing

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:07.670
the messages into Pub/Sub
as sales are happening.

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:09.890
DataFlow is doing a
very simple task here,

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:12.940
which is to transport
those messages from Pub/Sub

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:16.390
over to BigQuery using
that streaming API, which

00:29:16.390 --> 00:29:18.250
allows us to get
queries immediately

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:21.770
up-to-date on what's
happening within the business.

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:23.860
So when I first
started doing this--

00:29:23.860 --> 00:29:25.830
I've been at Google five years--

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:28.700
we tended to have to write
code to spin up any Dataflow

00:29:28.700 --> 00:29:30.040
pipeline.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:31.960
And that was even
for the simple stuff.

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:33.460
Here, since then,
we've actually got

00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:36.580
these fantastic templates,
which allow you to go to the UI,

00:29:36.580 --> 00:29:39.670
complete the template,
and it will automatically

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:41.530
transport the
information from Pub/Sub

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:44.255
to BigQuery, spinning up
Dataflow in the background.

00:29:44.255 --> 00:29:45.880
And to actually see
that in action, Win

00:29:45.880 --> 00:29:48.472
is going to show us a demo.

00:29:48.472 --> 00:29:49.392
WIN WOO: Yes, indeed.

00:29:49.392 --> 00:29:50.475
Thank you very much, Reza.

00:29:55.398 --> 00:29:55.898
OK.

00:29:55.898 --> 00:29:58.259
So let's go back to the
Cloud platform console.

00:29:58.259 --> 00:29:59.800
And there's a few
things that we need

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:02.320
to get the first
streaming pipeline that we

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:04.240
want to run working.

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:05.740
The first part is Pub/Sub.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:09.195
So let's go into the
Pub/Sub configuration.

00:30:09.195 --> 00:30:11.070
The first thing that we
need to do in Pub/Sub

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:13.420
is to create a Pub/Sub
topic that we'll

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:16.750
be using to read
or ingest messages

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:19.030
from a streaming source.

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:20.500
In this particular
case, we'll be

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:23.710
using a hypothetical
point-of-sale system

00:30:23.710 --> 00:30:27.040
that will be sending messages
to the Pub/Sub topic.

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.460
Let's go ahead and
create the topic.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:32.390
To do that, I click on
the Create Topic button,

00:30:32.390 --> 00:30:38.560
I give it a unique ID, like
a POS_DATA, click Create,

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:40.500
and there I have my new topic.

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:43.460
It is now ready to accept
streaming messages.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:45.130
The second part that
I need to perform

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:48.250
is to provision the
DataFlow pipeline.

00:30:48.250 --> 00:30:53.440
To do that, I can use one of
the pre-prepared templates that

00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:56.110
lets me create the
DataFlow pipeline,

00:30:56.110 --> 00:30:58.240
take the messages from
the Pub/Sub topic,

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:01.180
and then write them
into a BigQuery table.

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:05.320
In order to do that, here is an
existing template configuration

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:07.090
that I've already
pre-typed out so

00:31:07.090 --> 00:31:11.050
that you don't have to watch me
continually make syntax errors.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:13.480
All these details
are all you need

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:16.660
to have a simple pipeline
that takes the Pub/Sub

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:20.490
messages right to a BigQuery
table, all without code.

00:31:20.490 --> 00:31:25.920
I'm going to click on Run Job
here to provision the job.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:27.920
And the next screen
that I'll be taken to

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:31.580
will show the pipeline
being provisioned,

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:33.920
as well as a way to
visualize the actual DataFlow

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:36.190
graph itself.

00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:38.490
Now, because it's going to
take a little bit of time--

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:42.090
as in, maybe, 60, 90 seconds,
something like that--

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:44.040
to really get the
pipeline up and running,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:47.400
I'm going to go into a
pre-prepared pipeline which

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:50.130
already has data
streaming into it to show

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:52.560
you some of the more important
parts of the DataFlow

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:53.910
interface.

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.850
The first is, obviously,
the pipeline itself.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:00.900
What you see here, with the
different rectangles organized,

00:32:00.900 --> 00:32:03.750
as I said, as connected
nodes in a graph,

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:06.360
is actually the different
transformations and operations

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:09.510
on the data that the pipeline
is performing in order

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:12.630
to read the messages, do
some simple transformation,

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:14.700
and then write to
an output table.

00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:17.550
So the Pub/Sub
messages transformation

00:32:17.550 --> 00:32:19.620
is represented by
this box, which

00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:23.000
is for reading the
Pub/Sub messages.

00:32:23.000 --> 00:32:24.740
On the right-hand
side, you'll see

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:29.660
some interesting information
about the actual step itself.

00:32:29.660 --> 00:32:33.030
The step is parallelized across
different DataFlow workers,

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:35.820
which means that, because
we're running a distributed

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:39.570
service that can scale to,
really, any sort of data scale

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:42.240
that you want to throw
into the pipeline,

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:45.000
you can see what the
performance of the pipeline

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:47.670
is like in the context of
the number of different data

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:50.280
elements that have
been added in history

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:51.990
as well as the size
of all the elements

00:32:51.990 --> 00:32:55.610
that have been processed
by that particular step.

00:32:55.610 --> 00:32:58.210
Now, if I actually don't
click on any step at all,

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:01.220
you'll see some overall
statistics about the pipeline.

00:33:01.220 --> 00:33:02.955
So if you look
into auto-scaling,

00:33:02.955 --> 00:33:04.330
we can see that
currently there's

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:08.710
one worker operating
all the different steps

00:33:08.710 --> 00:33:10.096
of the pipeline itself.

00:33:10.096 --> 00:33:11.470
There's not a lot
of data flowing

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:13.040
inside the pipeline
at the moment,

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:16.150
so right now, one
worker is sufficient.

00:33:16.150 --> 00:33:18.180
As more data goes
into the pipeline,

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:21.820
the DataFlow service, as
a serverless offering,

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:24.010
will automatically scale
up the number of workers

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.500
to meet the volume
and throughput that

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:28.220
is needed by your application.

00:33:28.220 --> 00:33:31.360
So all that stuff is
without you needing

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:34.000
to configure any
infrastructure or to configure

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:37.160
any concerns about scaling.

00:33:37.160 --> 00:33:38.919
You can see within the
pipeline that there

00:33:38.919 --> 00:33:41.210
is other information shown,
like the number of elements

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:42.840
processed within each step.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:45.380
So for example, we have
24 elements a second

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:49.370
being written out to our
target table within BigQuery.

00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:50.594
So again, it's low volume.

00:33:50.594 --> 00:33:52.760
But that shows you some
more interesting information

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:56.800
about what your pipeline
is actually doing.

00:33:56.800 --> 00:33:58.990
Now that we've explained
the DataFlow pipeline,

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:01.120
let's actually look at the
output of the streaming

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:04.040
DataFlow pipeline itself, in the
context of the BigQuery table

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:05.510
it's being written to.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.620
So I'm going to go into the
BigQuery interface again.

00:34:08.620 --> 00:34:10.960
And to, again,
save me on typing,

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:14.050
I'm going to bring up a saved
query which I've pre-prepared

00:34:14.050 --> 00:34:18.460
ahead of time, and bring
up this particular query,

00:34:18.460 --> 00:34:21.440
which I will run very shortly.

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:23.590
But I've actually omitted
a particular part,

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:26.320
which is to actually get
some real-time data on stage

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:27.290
right now.

00:34:27.290 --> 00:34:30.820
So I'd like to, actually, bring
up Reza into the demonstration

00:34:30.820 --> 00:34:31.670
as well.

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:33.378
Actually, why don't
you stand here, Reza,

00:34:33.378 --> 00:34:37.388
because I think it's better
if you operate from there.

00:34:37.388 --> 00:34:39.179
I'm going to need to
do something inelegant

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:40.389
alt-tabbing here.

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:42.820
So I did say that this was a
point-of-sale demonstration,

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:45.290
so I did say it
was hypothetical,

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:47.290
but we did go to the
effort of actually building

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:50.389
a hypothetical, green screen
point-of-sale application,

00:34:50.389 --> 00:34:52.389
which, as you can see,
is very, very attractive.

00:34:52.389 --> 00:34:54.010
And Reza here is
going to help me

00:34:54.010 --> 00:34:58.420
purchase some products at one
of Bill's Grocer's stores.

00:34:58.420 --> 00:35:01.510
I'm an Australian, and I
always get very homesick

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:02.590
whenever I travel.

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:05.320
So I want to buy
some goodies here

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:09.561
to basically get through
the long days of the travel.

00:35:09.561 --> 00:35:10.560
REZA ROKNI: There we go.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:11.340
So that's one.

00:35:11.340 --> 00:35:13.376
WIN WOO: All right.

00:35:13.376 --> 00:35:15.720
