00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:30.610
taking data from real
time streaming source, all

00:36:30.610 --> 00:36:31.990
without code.

00:36:31.990 --> 00:36:32.866
Back to you, William.

00:36:32.866 --> 00:36:34.073
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE: All right.

00:36:34.073 --> 00:36:34.640
Thank you.

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:38.540
I'll switch back to the slides.

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:40.040
So what we saw here
is that pipeline

00:36:40.040 --> 00:36:42.320
where you can use
DataFlow without having

00:36:42.320 --> 00:36:43.320
to write any code--

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:43.820
sorry.

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:45.290
I'm clicking on
the wrong screen.

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:47.720
There we go-- without
having to write any code,

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:51.320
because the DataFlow
pipeline that was executing

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:54.770
has been pre-created for
a given task to bring data

00:36:54.770 --> 00:36:56.420
from Pub/Sub to BigQuery.

00:36:56.420 --> 00:36:59.060
Now, DataFlow can do
that, but DataFlow

00:36:59.060 --> 00:37:00.360
can do a lot more than that.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:03.170
It's really a full-fledged
processing system.

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:06.290
And so let's talk more about the
broader range of capabilities

00:37:06.290 --> 00:37:06.980
of DataFlow.

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:09.530
And again, going back
to this historical view,

00:37:09.530 --> 00:37:12.490
where DataFlow comes from
is really two things.

00:37:12.490 --> 00:37:16.190
FlumeJava is a system that
we developed at Google

00:37:16.190 --> 00:37:20.570
as a replacement for MapReduce,
a more efficient, easier way

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:24.110
to create and run large scale
data processing pipelines

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:25.280
in batch mode.

00:37:25.280 --> 00:37:27.740
In parallel,
another team created

00:37:27.740 --> 00:37:31.040
MillWheel which is a system
for stream processing.

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:34.340
Because at that time, there
was a clear move within Google

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:39.050
to real-time data processing,
and MillWheel met that need.

00:37:39.050 --> 00:37:40.820
In DataFlow, what
we've done is we've

00:37:40.820 --> 00:37:44.240
brought those two separate
services together and unified

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:46.490
them in DataFlow.

00:37:46.490 --> 00:37:48.530
So it's unified from a
programming perspective,

00:37:48.530 --> 00:37:53.180
where the same pipeline can
run in batch or in stream mode.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:55.610
It's also unified from the
execution environment, where

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:57.770
one service can
submit a pipeline

00:37:57.770 --> 00:37:58.920
and it runs both of them.

00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:01.580
And so that's really
the genesis of DataFlow.

00:38:01.580 --> 00:38:06.980
Having done that, and delivering
on that openness philosophy

00:38:06.980 --> 00:38:10.460
that I describe, we open
sourced the DataFlow SDK.

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:12.800
We gave it to the
Apache Foundation,

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:14.930
where it's now
called Apache Beam.

00:38:14.930 --> 00:38:18.200
And Beam-- the "B" of
"Beam" stands for batch,

00:38:18.200 --> 00:38:20.420
and the "EAM" stands for stream.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:22.610
And so, really, what
that name represents

00:38:22.610 --> 00:38:24.770
is a unification
of batch and stream

00:38:24.770 --> 00:38:26.930
in one programming model.

00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:29.240
And that's one thing that's
really, really important

00:38:29.240 --> 00:38:34.130
to Beam, because it maps
to a real need, where

00:38:34.130 --> 00:38:36.380
more and more, we
see companies move

00:38:36.380 --> 00:38:39.740
from a very batch-centric
view to a more real-time view.

00:38:39.740 --> 00:38:41.390
But that doesn't
happen overnight.

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:43.720
It's a progressive
process, where

00:38:43.720 --> 00:38:46.920
some of your sources, some
of your pipelines move.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:48.650
And when that happens,
you don't want

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.510
to have to re-implement
your business logic.

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:53.810
You just want to be able to take
your pipeline and say, hey--

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:58.015
you know, what used to be a file
drop every four hours is now

00:38:58.015 --> 00:38:59.480
a streaming pipeline.

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:02.472
Run the same business
logic on this data

00:39:02.472 --> 00:39:03.680
and don't make me rewrite it.

00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:05.960
And so Beam does that very well.

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:08.100
It also works the
other way around.

00:39:08.100 --> 00:39:10.640
I have a streaming pipeline,
but once in a while

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:13.520
I want to reprocess
historical information.

00:39:13.520 --> 00:39:14.600
No problem.

00:39:14.600 --> 00:39:16.490
Take the pipeline,
run it in batch

00:39:16.490 --> 00:39:18.500
mode against
historical information.

00:39:18.500 --> 00:39:23.690
And so that is something that is
a characteristic of Beam itself

00:39:23.690 --> 00:39:24.770
in Apache.

00:39:24.770 --> 00:39:28.160
And the Apache work is actually
not tied to Google in any way.

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:31.070
And so those Beam
pipelines that you write

00:39:31.070 --> 00:39:32.600
can run anywhere you want.

00:39:32.600 --> 00:39:35.000
They can run on
the Spark cluster.

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.420
They can run on other
Apache processing systems,

00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:40.580
like Flink, if you're
familiar with Flink,

00:39:40.580 --> 00:39:43.130
or just recently, our
friends at LinkedIn

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:45.289
open-sourced the Samza Runner.

00:39:45.289 --> 00:39:46.205
There is one for Apex.

00:39:46.205 --> 00:39:47.916
There is one for Gearpump.

00:39:47.916 --> 00:39:49.790
And so there are many
different systems where

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:51.500
you can run those pipelines.

00:39:51.500 --> 00:39:55.070
Google Cloud DataFlow
is just another one.

00:39:55.070 --> 00:39:57.470
And so the pipelines
are portable.

00:39:57.470 --> 00:39:59.900
If you run them on
DataFlow, what you get

00:39:59.900 --> 00:40:02.000
is the benefit of a
serverless environment.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.220
And so you don't have
to deploy a cluster

00:40:04.220 --> 00:40:05.780
and then run your pipelines.

00:40:05.780 --> 00:40:07.070
You just submit a pipeline.

00:40:07.070 --> 00:40:10.370
Just tell Google, "run
this for me in batch mode,"

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:12.470
or, "run this for
me in stream mode."

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:15.350
And Google automatically
provisions the right amount

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:18.710
of workers, continuously
auto-scales them

00:40:18.710 --> 00:40:20.420
through the life
of the pipeline,

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:21.792
and executes the pipeline.

00:40:21.792 --> 00:40:24.000
And you don't have to worry
about any infrastructure.

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:25.940
You just have to
write your pipeline,

00:40:25.940 --> 00:40:27.930
describe what you
want to get done.

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:30.740
And that's really
the core of DataFlow

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:33.290
from an operational perspective.

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:35.510
One thing I would
add is DataFlow

00:40:35.510 --> 00:40:38.600
is a very generic, very
versatile data processing

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:43.010
mechanism for any kind of
large scale data processing.

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:45.050
Data preparation
for machine learning

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:47.570
is one of the sweet spots.

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:50.960
In a way, in machine
learning, before you train,

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:52.550
you have to prepare your data.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:55.430
That typically
happens in batch mode.

00:40:55.430 --> 00:40:57.590
And then you train and
you create your model.

00:40:57.590 --> 00:41:00.470
When the time comes to use
your model for scoring,

00:41:00.470 --> 00:41:02.960
you have to take the
input data and prepare it

00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:05.810
in the exact same way that
you prepared it for training.

00:41:05.810 --> 00:41:08.300
You know, not doing
that is one of the most

00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:11.060
common sources of problems
with machine learning.

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:14.530
And so Beam allows you
to use that same pipeline

00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:16.300
at scoring time now.

00:41:16.300 --> 00:41:19.160
And scoring can happen
as a batch or stream.

00:41:19.160 --> 00:41:21.220
And so, again, you
get that flexibility.

00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:24.380
And that's the reason why,
internally, we really use

00:41:24.380 --> 00:41:26.830
the two together very commonly.

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:30.310
And we've built tools
on top of Apache Beam

00:41:30.310 --> 00:41:34.220
to make it easier to prepare
data for machine learning

00:41:34.220 --> 00:41:35.470
and prepare it for TensorFlow.

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:37.860
And those tools are being
open sourced as well.

00:41:37.860 --> 00:41:40.450
And so, again, Beam and
DataFlow are generic data

00:41:40.450 --> 00:41:44.440
processing services, but really
with a very strong sweet spot

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:47.050
for being used more and
more often in machine

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:48.410
learning context.

00:41:48.410 --> 00:41:52.530
And so, in a way, this is what
it looks like all put together.

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:56.240
In this case, the streaming
use case where data flows from

00:41:56.240 --> 00:41:59.920
whatever endpoint-- mobile
application, IOT devices--

00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:03.685
into an event bus, like Pub/Sub,
that we showed just earlier--

00:42:03.685 --> 00:42:08.260
you know, serverless event
bus, or Kafka, or others--

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:10.810
into DataFlow for stream
processing, and then

00:42:10.810 --> 00:42:14.050
straight into BigQuery
or other systems--

00:42:14.050 --> 00:42:17.080
you know, Bigtable or GCS--

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:18.500
and then use with ML.

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:22.300
So that's really where it fits
in that end-to-end pipeline.

00:42:22.300 --> 00:42:25.660
And Win is going to
show in more detail what

00:42:25.660 --> 00:42:27.550
DataFlow does in practice.

00:42:27.550 --> 00:42:29.220
WIN WOO: Absolutely.

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:31.920
So in the first pipeline,
we utilized a very simple

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:32.950
example--

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:34.620
reading messages
directly from Pub/Sub

00:42:34.620 --> 00:42:36.974
and then writing them
directly to a BigQuery table.

00:42:36.974 --> 00:42:39.390
In this particular example,
we're going to break the glass

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:41.556
and implement some more
sophisticated analytics that

00:42:41.556 --> 00:42:44.460
will be useful for
deriving insight later on,

00:42:44.460 --> 00:42:47.400
as well as for deriving
operational insights that we

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:51.410
can take advantage of in
the moment, as they occur.

00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:53.980
So you've heard a
lot about Pub/Sub

00:42:53.980 --> 00:42:57.140
as a streaming data
ingestion source.

00:42:57.140 --> 00:42:59.080
Pub/Sub can also
be used as a source

00:42:59.080 --> 00:43:01.780
to be written to that's the
output of a streaming data

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:02.950
pipeline.

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:05.627
Now, in this example,
I've enriched our pipeline

00:43:05.627 --> 00:43:07.210
with some additional
capabilities that

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:09.310
can leverage
Pub/Sub's capability

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:13.450
to notify downstream consumers
of useful information.

00:43:13.450 --> 00:43:17.160
The first of this is a
product lookup web service.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:22.720
The product lookup web service
enriches the purchased goods

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:25.270
information from the
checkout, and then adds

00:43:25.270 --> 00:43:26.700
additional information.

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:32.064
So it's an example of a way
to enrich incoming data,

00:43:32.064 --> 00:43:33.730
and then add additional
information that

00:43:33.730 --> 00:43:36.610
comes from external sources, and
then write that information out

00:43:36.610 --> 00:43:40.111
to either a batch source
or to a streaming source.

00:43:40.111 --> 00:43:41.860
The second piece of
information that we'll

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:45.310
be incorporating via external
sources into the pipeline

00:43:45.310 --> 00:43:47.330
is an inventory web service.

00:43:47.330 --> 00:43:49.360
And this could
potentially hold things

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:53.650
like stock level and other
inventory-related information.

00:43:53.650 --> 00:43:56.650
Now, as purchases are being
made from the point-of-sale

00:43:56.650 --> 00:43:59.380
checkout, we're going to be
integrating with the inventory

00:43:59.380 --> 00:44:01.660
web service to update
some information,

00:44:01.660 --> 00:44:03.910
such as the stock
level for product,

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:07.420
as well as to produce alerts
on particular products that are

00:44:07.420 --> 00:44:09.500
seeing spikes in consumption--

00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:13.210
which may be an indicator of a
stock out situation in store,

00:44:13.210 --> 00:44:14.870
hypothetically.

00:44:14.870 --> 00:44:18.287
So with that in mind, I'd like
to go into the demo to show you

00:44:18.287 --> 00:44:19.370
what that might look like.

00:44:24.820 --> 00:44:26.710
So this next screen
that I'm bringing up

00:44:26.710 --> 00:44:29.770
is our new enhanced pipeline.

00:44:29.770 --> 00:44:31.360
And you can simply
see, by counting

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:33.400
the number of rectangles
on the screen,

00:44:33.400 --> 00:44:36.340
it must be doing something
pretty cool, right?

00:44:36.340 --> 00:44:38.620
So I'm going to
not talk about all

00:44:38.620 --> 00:44:40.840
the different transformations
that we're applying.

00:44:40.840 --> 00:44:43.820
This is producing some
analytical insights as well as

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:45.410
some operational insights.

00:44:45.410 --> 00:44:49.540
But I'm going to talk about the
real-time stock notification

00:44:49.540 --> 00:44:51.050
that I've talked about earlier.

00:44:51.050 --> 00:44:53.350
So how can we notify
Bill's Grocer's stores

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:56.170
whenever there's a threshold
of a particular product

00:44:56.170 --> 00:44:57.850
that's been exceeded--

00:44:57.850 --> 00:45:00.336
being purchased in a
short period of time?

00:45:00.336 --> 00:45:01.960
So let's see how that
actually happens.

00:45:06.380 --> 00:45:09.020
So first off, we still need
to read the information.

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:11.830
So instead of reading individual
purchase information now,

00:45:11.830 --> 00:45:13.570
we're actually reading
whole invoices,

00:45:13.570 --> 00:45:15.629
where there's one or
more purchases within it.

00:45:15.629 --> 00:45:17.170
When we're doing
that, we can perform

00:45:17.170 --> 00:45:19.810
more interesting analytics,
things like, obviously,

00:45:19.810 --> 00:45:21.820
what is the average
size of an order,

00:45:21.820 --> 00:45:24.570
and other information like that.

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:26.740
After we read the
information, we

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:29.470
can augment that information
with additional information

00:45:29.470 --> 00:45:32.350
from our product lookup service,
which is where I'm actually

00:45:32.350 --> 00:45:34.510
hovering over right now.

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:36.860
I want to focus on the
operational insights

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:39.430
that we can enable
by detecting spikes

00:45:39.430 --> 00:45:42.310
in purchasing activity
for particular products.

00:45:42.310 --> 00:45:46.230
And this is the part of the
pipeline which does that.

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:49.650
In order to understand
statistics and information that

00:45:49.650 --> 00:45:52.410
occur within a
period of time, we

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:55.050
apply a technique
called windowing.

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:58.230
In this particular case, it's
a sliding window aggregation

00:45:58.230 --> 00:46:02.010
across streaming data that
includes timestamp information

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:05.040
that allows us to then count
the number of occurrences

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:08.170
of a particular purchase
over that period of time,

00:46:08.170 --> 00:46:10.110
and then, if it exceeds
a certain threshold,

00:46:10.110 --> 00:46:13.110
write a particular message
to a Pub/Sub topic,

00:46:13.110 --> 00:46:18.910
which is then used by downstream
consumers to display an alert.

00:46:18.910 --> 00:46:21.500
So that's basically the
"write alerts to Pub"--

00:46:21.500 --> 00:46:23.910
actually, it should
be "Pub/Sub"--

00:46:23.910 --> 00:46:26.410
that's what that particular
transformation is doing.

00:46:26.410 --> 00:46:28.740
So I think, at this
point, it might

00:46:28.740 --> 00:46:30.450
be more useful to
actually show this

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:33.220
in action in our
hypothetical store scenario.

