OK.

00:47:58.990 --> 00:47:59.710
Good.

00:47:59.710 --> 00:48:00.360
It's been three days.

00:48:00.360 --> 00:48:02.610
We've been looking at this
packet and not touching it.

00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:07.890
So having done the
DataFlow demo-- one thing I

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:08.790
forgot to mention.

00:48:08.790 --> 00:48:13.270
DataFlow supports
Java, Python, Scala,

00:48:13.270 --> 00:48:16.000
that our friends at
Spotify contributed.

00:48:16.000 --> 00:48:17.610
There's a Go SDK
that's almost ready.

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:20.340
So there's a choice
of languages here.

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:22.410
And, yeah-- I see the
biscuits are passing.

00:48:22.410 --> 00:48:22.910
See?

00:48:22.910 --> 00:48:24.576
That's why you want
to sit in the front.

00:48:24.576 --> 00:48:27.914
You get the biscuits, so
lesson learned for next time.

00:48:27.914 --> 00:48:29.330
Let's talk about
another service--

00:48:29.330 --> 00:48:30.910
DataProc.

00:48:30.910 --> 00:48:33.380
So the services we've
talked about so far--

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:37.130
Pub/Sub, BigQuery, DataFlow--
those are cognitive services,

00:48:37.130 --> 00:48:40.580
really built from scratch to run
in a serverless mode on Google

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:41.480
Cloud.

00:48:41.480 --> 00:48:44.810
Obviously, there's also a
lot of very valuable systems

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:46.220
in the open source community.

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:49.430
And in the bigger data
space, Spark and Hadoop

00:48:49.430 --> 00:48:51.320
are really two
that come to mind.

00:48:51.320 --> 00:48:54.380
And we want to make sure
that customers on GCP

00:48:54.380 --> 00:48:57.390
can use whatever the
right tool for the job is.

00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:59.940
And so if the right
tool is Spark or Hadoop,

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:02.060
because of some
library, some tool,

00:49:02.060 --> 00:49:05.870
or they've written already a lot
of pipelines on-prem and want

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:10.370
to run that on GCP, DataProc
makes that very easy by taking

00:49:10.370 --> 00:49:12.670
Hadoop and Spark-- the
same Hadoop and Spark,

00:49:12.670 --> 00:49:15.450
the same bits from
Apache everyone uses--

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:20.060
and deploying them, deploying
a cluster in about 90 seconds.

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:21.500
By the way, you
pay by the second.

00:49:21.500 --> 00:49:23.130
And so those clusters--

00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:25.550
you know, it's a normal
Hadoop and Spark cluster,

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:27.470
but it's also a very
dynamic cluster.

00:49:27.470 --> 00:49:29.390
You can start and
you can stop it.

00:49:29.390 --> 00:49:32.750
And what it changes is
the way people typically

00:49:32.750 --> 00:49:35.330
use it on Cloud,
where they don't

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:39.210
have those long-lived clusters
that people have to share,

00:49:39.210 --> 00:49:42.920
and you have to update
them, and agree on version.

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:45.160
It's a much more
dynamic way of getting

00:49:45.160 --> 00:49:48.590
a cluster for whatever job
you want to run, and keep it

00:49:48.590 --> 00:49:51.470
for one job, for one
day, for one week,

00:49:51.470 --> 00:49:54.680
and really free yourself
from all the administration

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:58.220
while keeping access to the
power, and the ecosystem,

00:49:58.220 --> 00:50:00.350
and all the tools available.

00:50:00.350 --> 00:50:02.750
And so when you use it, you
can see on the left side

00:50:02.750 --> 00:50:05.330
our Jobs API, where
you can run your jobs,

00:50:05.330 --> 00:50:09.380
and you can write them using all
the common tools in the Spark

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:12.380
and Hadoop ecosystem,
and those jobs

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:16.330
execute on all the same
data storage systems

00:50:16.330 --> 00:50:17.280
that we talked about.

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:21.200
And so I mentioned the clusters
come up in about 90 seconds.

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:24.580
When the cluster is up, you
can start working right away.

00:50:24.580 --> 00:50:28.580
The next step is not
bring data to the cluster.

00:50:28.580 --> 00:50:30.820
And again, remember, no silo.

00:50:30.820 --> 00:50:34.430
Those Hadoop and Spark
clusters can execute on data

00:50:34.430 --> 00:50:37.110
in BigQuery, on data in
Google Cloud Storage,

00:50:37.110 --> 00:50:39.710
on data in Bigtable directly.

00:50:39.710 --> 00:50:44.180
And that really lets you mix and
match, across your environment,

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:46.500
services like
BigQuery and DataFlow,

00:50:46.500 --> 00:50:49.820
whether cloud native, with
Hadoop and Spark using

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:52.340
DataProc, and use
that all together.

00:50:52.340 --> 00:50:54.520
You know, again, use the
right tool for the job.

00:50:54.520 --> 00:50:57.260
And so that's a really
powerful part of DataProc.

00:50:57.260 --> 00:50:59.759
And let's look at
it in practice.

00:50:59.759 --> 00:51:00.550
WIN WOO: All right.

00:51:00.550 --> 00:51:02.810
So soon, again.

00:51:02.810 --> 00:51:03.310
OK.

00:51:03.310 --> 00:51:07.480
So I think we're done with
our checkout, so I can close

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:11.980
this window, and, instead, show
you the DataProc console inside

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:13.990
of the Cloud Platform console.

00:51:13.990 --> 00:51:17.320
So here we're seeing the
DataProc list of clusters.

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:19.580
And I've got one
pre-prepared here.

00:51:19.580 --> 00:51:21.760
But because DataProc
starts up very quickly,

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:25.200
I'm going to create
a cluster on the fly.

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:28.347
So let's give it a slightly
unique name, like so.

00:51:28.347 --> 00:51:30.680
I'm going to leave these
sensible defaults, for the most

00:51:30.680 --> 00:51:33.920
part, but maybe change some
specific things I'll call out

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:36.734
when I'm creating
my DataProc cluster.

00:51:36.734 --> 00:51:38.150
So the first thing
is maybe I want

00:51:38.150 --> 00:51:40.600
to specify a particular
region where it runs.

00:51:40.600 --> 00:51:42.770
So all my data is
in US Central One,

00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:45.800
so it makes sense to do that.

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:47.600
We're using the
sensible defaults

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:50.660
that I have for a very simple
job that I'm going to spin up,

00:51:50.660 --> 00:51:53.430
which is two worker nodes.

00:51:53.430 --> 00:51:55.180
I'm going to click
into Advanced Options

00:51:55.180 --> 00:51:57.340
here before creating my cluster.

00:51:57.340 --> 00:52:00.790
And the first thing I want
to do in the advanced options

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:03.700
is to allow API
access from my cluster

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:07.240
so that my cluster can call
different Google Cloud Platform

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:09.700
services to do
interesting things,

00:52:09.700 --> 00:52:13.450
as well as install some
additional dependencies, which

00:52:13.450 --> 00:52:15.400
I'll ultimately need for my job.

00:52:15.400 --> 00:52:18.100
So I click on Browse.

00:52:18.100 --> 00:52:21.340
I'm going to point my
DataProc configuration

00:52:21.340 --> 00:52:23.380
at a particular script
that I want DataProc

00:52:23.380 --> 00:52:26.660
to run, to install
some dependencies,

00:52:26.660 --> 00:52:28.790
and then click on Select.

00:52:28.790 --> 00:52:31.850
And from here, I should
be able to click on Create

00:52:31.850 --> 00:52:36.170
and have my cluster spun up
with my custom dependencies

00:52:36.170 --> 00:52:39.820
available on each worker.

00:52:39.820 --> 00:52:42.070
So to this point in
time, I've not actually

00:52:42.070 --> 00:52:44.600
described what my
job actually does.

00:52:44.600 --> 00:52:50.110
So let me rectify that
particular situation.

00:52:50.110 --> 00:52:51.940
Actually, I can
click on Buckets here

00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:54.390
to show you this information.

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:57.430
Inside of our project, we have
some hypothetical customer

00:52:57.430 --> 00:52:59.440
feedback that some
customers have

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:03.940
sent to us, of which we're
going to try and analyze

00:53:03.940 --> 00:53:07.390
using a batch processing job
with our Cloud Natural Language

00:53:07.390 --> 00:53:10.360
API so we can understand the
sentiment of those emails

00:53:10.360 --> 00:53:13.300
and, thereby, be informed
of some actions to take,

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:16.990
because there's a potentially
unsatisfied customer

00:53:16.990 --> 00:53:19.450
that we want to keep in touch
with or get in touch with.

00:53:19.450 --> 00:53:22.180
So let's have a look at
the emails, or the input.

00:53:22.180 --> 00:53:27.040
So we're in the Spark directory
here, which contains my input.

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:29.680
I've got two emails
here as text files.

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:32.200
And as you can imagine,
these text files actually

00:53:32.200 --> 00:53:35.110
contain some
angry-sounding words in it,

00:53:35.110 --> 00:53:37.550
and there's some customer
dissatisfaction there.

00:53:37.550 --> 00:53:39.550
Let me just click on that
just to prove the fact

00:53:39.550 --> 00:53:41.710
that this particular person--

00:53:41.710 --> 00:53:43.876
Bob-- is not very happy.

00:53:43.876 --> 00:53:45.250
But you can kind
of get the sense

00:53:45.250 --> 00:53:48.010
of what's within these emails
and the sort of sentiment

00:53:48.010 --> 00:53:52.280
that our batch processing job
should actually come back with.

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:55.560
And, of course, I
have my code as well.

00:53:55.560 --> 00:53:58.040
So we're not here to basically
step through Python, so I

00:53:58.040 --> 00:54:00.410
won't actually show you that.

00:54:00.410 --> 00:54:03.230
But know that this is the
piece of code that I will then

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:05.890
run by submitting a Spark job--

00:54:05.890 --> 00:54:08.690
or a PySpark job, more
specifically-- to the cluster.

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:21.220
So that was the data
that I want to show.

00:54:21.220 --> 00:54:24.770
In order to submit a
job, I can actually just

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:30.710
click on a previous job here
and clone its configuration.

00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:34.220
I want to do that because I
have all my previous parameters

00:54:34.220 --> 00:54:35.150
already provided.

00:54:35.150 --> 00:54:36.560
So I don't need to type them in.

00:54:36.560 --> 00:54:39.740
You can see that
my Python file is

00:54:39.740 --> 00:54:41.420
in this particular parameter.

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:46.130
I've got my input pointing
to the input directory, which

00:54:46.130 --> 00:54:48.680
contains my emails,
and I want the output

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:54.170
to be sentiment analysis output
on each line in each email,

00:54:54.170 --> 00:54:55.684
written to a CSV file.

00:54:58.449 --> 00:54:59.990
Of course, the most
important element

00:54:59.990 --> 00:55:01.490
is actually choosing
the cluster.

00:55:01.490 --> 00:55:02.910
And the other clusters
actually come up now,

00:55:02.910 --> 00:55:04.700
so that's why I've
got two available.

00:55:04.700 --> 00:55:07.760
I'm going to choose this
particular cluster, click

00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:08.940
on Submit--

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:09.440
oh.

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:10.480
Whoops.

00:55:10.480 --> 00:55:12.710
I actually have to change
the actual job name

00:55:12.710 --> 00:55:17.280
because it needs to
be unique, like so.

00:55:17.280 --> 00:55:23.070
I'll click on Submit, and
my job should be running.

00:55:23.070 --> 00:55:24.690
Now, if I click
into the actual job,

00:55:24.690 --> 00:55:26.760
the streaming logging
output from Spark

00:55:26.760 --> 00:55:28.502
should actually be displayed.

00:55:28.502 --> 00:55:30.960
You'll see-- actually, it's
probably too small to see here.

00:55:30.960 --> 00:55:33.840
But one interesting
aspect of DataProc

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:37.380
is its ability to talk directly
to Google Cloud Storage, much

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:40.230
like a normal HTFS data source.

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:42.540
So there's no code changes
that you need in order

00:55:42.540 --> 00:55:45.420
to run a job on DataProc
and have your data stored

00:55:45.420 --> 00:55:47.310
on Cloud Storage.

00:55:47.310 --> 00:55:49.200
And you get all the
scaling benefits

00:55:49.200 --> 00:55:52.677
of Google Cloud Storage as
an object storage source.

00:55:52.677 --> 00:55:54.510
So I think now the job
is actually complete,

00:55:54.510 --> 00:55:56.039
because I only have two emails.

00:55:56.039 --> 00:55:58.080
In order to prove the
point, I'm going to go back

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:00.240
to my directory here.

00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:02.670
And now there's an
output directory inside

00:56:02.670 --> 00:56:06.210
of my emails bucket, and here's
the output of my analysis

00:56:06.210 --> 00:56:07.770
with the batch processing job.

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:10.440
I'm going to actually leave the
output of the Natural Language

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:12.470
API as a discussion
for Reza, who

00:56:12.470 --> 00:56:14.640
will talk about extending
this with some additional

00:56:14.640 --> 00:56:16.290
capabilities.

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:17.260
So thank you, Reza.

00:56:20.585 --> 00:56:24.990
REZA ROKNI: So one of
the interesting things

00:56:24.990 --> 00:56:28.770
that Win highlighted
was the use of an API

00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:31.380
to start extracting
some structure

00:56:31.380 --> 00:56:32.880
from unstructured content.

00:56:32.880 --> 00:56:35.096
And actually, when you're
building out on a platform,

00:56:35.096 --> 00:56:36.720
you're investing in
that platform, what

00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:38.850
you want that platform
to do is provide you

00:56:38.850 --> 00:56:41.810
with all of the tools that you
need to do all of your work.

00:56:41.810 --> 00:56:45.270
So a platform is a group of
technology used as a base

00:56:45.270 --> 00:56:47.430
to build other things on top of.

00:56:47.430 --> 00:56:50.580
And some of the other
tools that are available--

00:56:50.580 --> 00:56:53.010
and we show a small
piece of it there--

00:56:53.010 --> 00:56:55.020
is our machine
learning capabilities.

00:56:55.020 --> 00:56:58.650
So things like Speech
Detect, Cloud Translation,

00:56:58.650 --> 00:57:00.390
Natural Language
Understanding, these

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:03.120
are all APIs that you
want to be able to access

00:57:03.120 --> 00:57:08.040
with your processing tools to
be able to extract information

00:57:08.040 --> 00:57:10.380
from that wealth of
unstructured content.

00:57:10.380 --> 00:57:14.280
Now, let's expand the
use case that we just

00:57:14.280 --> 00:57:18.442
saw around the providing
sentiment against an email.

00:57:18.442 --> 00:57:20.400
Now, this is actually
based off a real use case

00:57:20.400 --> 00:57:22.891
with a retail customer.

00:57:22.891 --> 00:57:24.390
Let's imagine you're
getting emails.

00:57:24.390 --> 00:57:26.723
And what you want to do is,
when someone is particularly

00:57:26.723 --> 00:57:29.580
angry or upset, you
would like to put them

00:57:29.580 --> 00:57:31.290
onto a priority queue.

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:33.720
Because retaining
customers is expensive.

00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:35.676
Losing customers is even worse.

00:57:35.676 --> 00:57:37.050
And what you don't
want to do is,

00:57:37.050 --> 00:57:39.540
if there's something that you
could remediate the situation

00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:42.030
with-- for example, a
simple set of vouchers,

00:57:42.030 --> 00:57:43.740
or a simple phone
call or an email--

00:57:43.740 --> 00:57:45.217
to actually keep
that customer, you

00:57:45.217 --> 00:57:47.050
should put them on top
of the priority list.

00:57:47.050 --> 00:57:50.490
And one of our customers has
an amazing return on investment

00:57:50.490 --> 00:57:53.209
by doing just this activity.

00:57:53.209 --> 00:57:54.750
But we need to add
a little bit more.

00:57:54.750 --> 00:57:57.750
So we can extract sentiment
using Natural Language

00:57:57.750 --> 00:57:59.460
Processing API.

00:57:59.460 --> 00:58:01.980
But we also-- because we're
storing this information

00:58:01.980 --> 00:58:04.860
in other system,
we want to not have

00:58:04.860 --> 00:58:07.230
the burden of actually taking
unnecessary information

00:58:07.230 --> 00:58:08.410
into the other system.

00:58:08.410 --> 00:58:11.040
So by that I mean, if, for
example, we have the user's

00:58:11.040 --> 00:58:13.830
name, their email, they've
accidentally put their credit

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:16.230
card, we'd like help
to actually remove

00:58:16.230 --> 00:58:18.210
this information
from that content

00:58:18.210 --> 00:58:20.370
before we store
it somewhere else.

00:58:20.370 --> 00:58:23.130
You know, we want to avoid
unnecessary proliferation

00:58:23.130 --> 00:58:24.744
of PII information.

00:58:24.744 --> 00:58:26.160
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:58:26.160 --> 00:58:28.590
going to add another piece,
which is the Data Loss

00:58:28.590 --> 00:58:29.980
Prevention API.

00:58:29.980 --> 00:58:32.910
So this uses things
like machine learning,

00:58:32.910 --> 00:58:36.760
pattern matching,
mathematical checksums,

00:58:36.760 --> 00:58:41.580
et cetera, to actually help
find sensitive information.

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:45.900
And then what it does is it
allows you to remove or star

00:58:45.900 --> 00:58:49.720
over that information as you
store it in another location.

00:58:49.720 --> 00:58:54.780
So what we're going to do now is
go through and just demo this.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:59:03.220
What we're going to do is show
a particular DataFlow pipeline.

00:59:03.220 --> 00:59:06.800
So we saw DataProc before with
the Natural Language Processing

00:59:06.800 --> 00:59:07.560
API.

00:59:07.560 --> 00:59:11.100
Here we have a DataFlow pipeline
that's going to be taking--

00:59:11.100 --> 00:59:14.490
whenever a file lands in
Google Cloud Storage--

00:59:14.490 --> 00:59:15.859
let's assume that's an email--

00:59:15.859 --> 00:59:17.400
it's going to take
that file and it's

00:59:17.400 --> 00:59:19.350
going to run it through
the two processes.

00:59:19.350 --> 00:59:21.940
It's going to run it through
the Data Loss Prevention API,

00:59:21.940 --> 00:59:24.240
and then the Natural
Language Understanding

00:59:24.240 --> 00:59:26.274
API-- the NLP API.

00:59:26.274 --> 00:59:27.690
And at the end of
that, it's going

00:59:27.690 --> 00:59:30.480
to store that
information in BigQuery.

00:59:30.480 --> 00:59:34.810
So right now, this is
processing up to three files.

00:59:34.810 --> 00:59:37.440
So I'm going to go to
Google Cloud Storage,

00:59:37.440 --> 00:59:40.950
go to our email
folder, go to Inbound,

00:59:40.950 --> 00:59:45.570
and I do have an item ready--

00:59:45.570 --> 00:59:49.250
upload files--
this angry British.

00:59:49.250 --> 00:59:51.020
Obviously, there's
no swearing in it.

00:59:51.020 --> 00:59:52.880
We don't do swearing in Britain.

00:59:52.880 --> 00:59:54.500
So we've got an
angry British person

00:59:54.500 --> 00:59:57.410
who's going to update
some information.

00:59:57.410 --> 01:00:00.570
That has been uploaded
to this cloud storage.

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:04.677
If I go to our thing,
we're still at three.

01:00:04.677 --> 01:00:07.010
What this will do is, when
it detects that that file has

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:10.400
landed, it's going to process
it and land it over here,

01:00:10.400 --> 01:00:13.980
in this BigQuery table.

01:00:13.980 --> 01:00:16.280
So I'm just going to
run this query here.

01:00:16.280 --> 01:00:18.150
You can see a fourth
element has been added.

01:00:18.150 --> 01:00:21.560
If I go back, we can see that
that incremented it up to four.

01:00:21.560 --> 01:00:22.940
So that's been processed.

01:00:22.940 --> 01:00:25.110
Let's look at what it's done.

01:00:25.110 --> 01:00:27.650
So I think it was
this one that landed.

01:00:27.650 --> 01:00:30.900
So here, there's a very
angry person who's--

01:00:30.900 --> 01:00:33.200
the birthday cake for their
daughter's birthday party

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:35.180
arrived late.

01:00:35.180 --> 01:00:38.840
It has redacted information,
like their credit card, which

01:00:38.840 --> 01:00:42.320
they had put into the piece.

01:00:42.320 --> 01:00:44.360
And it also redacted their name.

01:00:44.360 --> 01:00:47.310
It was actually Reza, there.

01:00:47.310 --> 01:00:48.930
We also have a sentiment score.

01:00:48.930 --> 01:00:52.850
So this is negative four
with a high magnitude.

01:00:52.850 --> 01:00:56.060
In other words, they were
quite cross about having

01:00:56.060 --> 01:00:58.670
their birthday cake
arrive a day late.

01:00:58.670 --> 01:01:00.620
We're almost there
in terms of having

01:01:00.620 --> 01:01:02.070
a full footwork for a pipeline.

01:01:02.070 --> 01:01:04.850
We've got-- as emails are coming
in, we're able to do this work

01:01:04.850 --> 01:01:06.852
and add them into BigQuery.

01:01:06.852 --> 01:01:08.810
But what we haven't got
is that final piece of,

01:01:08.810 --> 01:01:12.565
how do we notify our support
people to actually go and do

01:01:12.565 --> 01:01:13.440
something about this?

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:15.350
And for that, we
need a bit of glue

01:01:15.350 --> 01:01:17.540
to stick several
processes together.

01:01:17.540 --> 01:01:19.222
So over to William, quickly.

01:01:19.222 --> 01:01:20.430
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE: Thank you.

01:01:20.430 --> 01:01:22.070
And we will switch
back to slide.

01:01:22.070 --> 01:01:25.060
And so I think "glue" is a
good term as we describe it,

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:27.490
because we've been talking
about individual services

01:01:27.490 --> 01:01:28.780
and what they can do.

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:32.770
But data processing is really
an end-to-end experience,

01:01:32.770 --> 01:01:36.040
and you need something that
ties together those processing

01:01:36.040 --> 01:01:39.040
systems, and gives you
control and visibility

01:01:39.040 --> 01:01:41.180
and a unified point
of management.

01:01:41.180 --> 01:01:43.630
And that's where Cloud
Composer comes in.

01:01:43.630 --> 01:01:46.380
Composer is one of the newest
services on the platform.

01:01:46.380 --> 01:01:49.900
It went generally
available this week.

01:01:49.900 --> 01:01:53.110
It is a managed version
of Apache Airflow.

01:01:53.110 --> 01:01:55.000
So if your familiar
with Apache Airflow--

01:01:55.000 --> 01:01:59.260
which is, right now, in
the open source community,

01:01:59.260 --> 01:02:01.960
the acquisition service
with the most momentum,

01:02:01.960 --> 01:02:03.580
really, really loved by users.

01:02:03.580 --> 01:02:08.040
And so we chose to use it,
make it available as a managed

01:02:08.040 --> 01:02:11.620
service on the platform,
contribute to Airflow, bringing

01:02:11.620 --> 01:02:15.280
operators that allow it to
act on all the relevant Google

01:02:15.280 --> 01:02:19.000
Cloud Services, and make that
available with no deployment

01:02:19.000 --> 01:02:23.830
to all our users, to easily
orchestrate and manage data

01:02:23.830 --> 01:02:25.360
processing on Google Cloud.

01:02:25.360 --> 01:02:27.650
And so let's see what it
looks like in practice.

01:02:27.650 --> 01:02:28.556
Reza?

01:02:28.556 --> 01:02:29.181
REZA ROKNI: OK.

01:02:29.181 --> 01:02:30.940
So what we're going
to do now is add

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:33.200
that final piece,
which is orchestrate

01:02:33.200 --> 01:02:36.790
moving the information that
exists in BigQuery into a CSV

01:02:36.790 --> 01:02:40.160
file ready for
our support folks.

01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:43.640
So I'm going to use
Google Airflow here--

01:02:43.640 --> 01:02:44.690
sorry.

01:02:44.690 --> 01:02:48.040
Google Composer, which is
running an Airflow job.

01:02:48.040 --> 01:02:50.680
The DAG that I have
in mind is going

01:02:50.680 --> 01:02:53.440
to do a query against
that BigQuery table,

01:02:53.440 --> 01:02:56.050
look at the last
two hours, and then

01:02:56.050 --> 01:02:58.570
its going to put the results
of that in another table,

01:02:58.570 --> 01:03:01.810
ready to be exported
out as a CSV file.

01:03:01.810 --> 01:03:02.560
So-- oh.

01:03:02.560 --> 01:03:04.101
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE:
You show the demo.

01:03:04.101 --> 01:03:05.430
REZA ROKNI: So sorry.

01:03:05.430 --> 01:03:06.660
Now I'll show you the-- yeah.

01:03:06.660 --> 01:03:07.930
I forgot to hit the button.

01:03:07.930 --> 01:03:09.110
Apologies for that.

01:03:09.110 --> 01:03:11.740
So here we have the Airflow DAG.

01:03:11.740 --> 01:03:13.290
This is the Notify Job.

01:03:13.290 --> 01:03:15.310
If you can see, it
comes in two steps--

01:03:15.310 --> 01:03:16.970
Recent Query, Query,
and then Export.

01:03:16.970 --> 01:03:19.300
So I'm going to go and--

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:21.700
even though this DAG
has been scheduled

01:03:21.700 --> 01:03:25.300
to run every two hours, I'm
going to just kick it off

01:03:25.300 --> 01:03:28.090
by triggering the process now.

01:03:28.090 --> 01:03:31.180
And in a moment, we'll see these
numbers-- we see it already,

01:03:31.180 --> 01:03:33.070
that the job's been queued.

01:03:33.070 --> 01:03:35.170
In a moment, we'll see
both of those tasks being

01:03:35.170 --> 01:03:37.840
processed successfully
and the move happening.

01:03:37.840 --> 01:03:41.090
What that will do is,
it will land a file--

01:03:41.090 --> 01:03:42.420
