01:12:24.470 --> 01:12:27.980
So this is very quick, nice and
easy Google Sheets integration

01:12:27.980 --> 01:12:30.110
with BigQuery.

01:12:30.110 --> 01:12:30.610
OK.

01:12:33.210 --> 01:12:34.970
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE:
Thank you, Reza.

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:38.090
Let's switch back to the slides.

01:12:38.090 --> 01:12:42.630
And so, having shown an overview
of the platform and services

01:12:42.630 --> 01:12:44.500
like Pub/Sub, BigQuery,
DataFlow, DataProc,

01:12:44.500 --> 01:12:47.640
we here use this
simplified example,

01:12:47.640 --> 01:12:51.780
with this pseudo point-of-sale
and simplified example,

01:12:51.780 --> 01:12:55.230
really just to show what
each product does in a very

01:12:55.230 --> 01:12:57.540
concrete and applied way.

01:12:57.540 --> 01:13:00.420
But now, let's look at
a real life scenario

01:13:00.420 --> 01:13:03.380
and how that's being used
by, in this case, Moloco.

01:13:03.380 --> 01:13:07.200
And Hooyeon Lee is joining us
to talk about their experience

01:13:07.200 --> 01:13:08.365
on the platform.

01:13:08.365 --> 01:13:11.215
[APPLAUSE]

01:13:12.920 --> 01:13:15.170
HOOYEON LEE: Thank you,
William, for the introduction.

01:13:15.170 --> 01:13:16.280
My name is Hooyeon Lee.

01:13:16.280 --> 01:13:18.350
I'm a research
scientist at Moloco.

01:13:18.350 --> 01:13:22.442
I've been with Moloco
for about two years.

01:13:22.442 --> 01:13:26.050
The green button?

01:13:26.050 --> 01:13:26.550
Yeah.

01:13:26.550 --> 01:13:30.590
So I'll briefly talk
about the company, Moloco,

01:13:30.590 --> 01:13:31.590
and what we do.

01:13:31.590 --> 01:13:34.190
So we are a mobile
ad tech company

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:37.070
that turns big data into ad
performance using machine

01:13:37.070 --> 01:13:38.180
learning models.

01:13:38.180 --> 01:13:42.140
Our clients use us for marketing
purposes, like user acquisition

01:13:42.140 --> 01:13:46.160
and reengagement, as well as
monetization of their products.

01:13:46.160 --> 01:13:48.230
Our cloud solution
is deeply integrated

01:13:48.230 --> 01:13:49.670
with Google Cloud Platform.

01:13:49.670 --> 01:13:52.700
So in particular, we use the
Bigtable, BigQuery, DataFlow,

01:13:52.700 --> 01:13:53.360
and Pub/Sub--

01:13:53.360 --> 01:13:58.140
the products that you
saw in this session.

01:13:58.140 --> 01:14:01.810
Some of the challenges we faced
while building a scalable ad

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:06.080
cloud solution can be summarized
as these three things.

01:14:06.080 --> 01:14:08.810
And I'm going to talk
about how we have overcome

01:14:08.810 --> 01:14:11.670
these challenges using some of
the products of Google Cloud

01:14:11.670 --> 01:14:12.650
Platform.

01:14:12.650 --> 01:14:14.780
So the first thing
that comes to my mind

01:14:14.780 --> 01:14:17.900
is the small latency
requirement with high volume

01:14:17.900 --> 01:14:19.070
of network traffic.

01:14:19.070 --> 01:14:23.360
So for mobile advertisement,
as soon as a user opens an app

01:14:23.360 --> 01:14:26.000
and that shows an ad
like an image or video,

01:14:26.000 --> 01:14:29.030
we receive an event that
is called a bit request

01:14:29.030 --> 01:14:31.040
from the bit exchange servers.

01:14:31.040 --> 01:14:33.650
So those are
third-party services

01:14:33.650 --> 01:14:34.820
that we are integrated with.

01:14:34.820 --> 01:14:37.170
And as soon as we
receive a bit request,

01:14:37.170 --> 01:14:39.830
we have to respond
within 100 milliseconds.

01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:41.360
We have to determine
whether we want

01:14:41.360 --> 01:14:43.250
to participate in
this real-time auction

01:14:43.250 --> 01:14:45.980
or not, and how much price
we're willing to pay,

01:14:45.980 --> 01:14:48.360
and what kind of image or
videos we want to serve,

01:14:48.360 --> 01:14:50.600
and if the user
clicks on it, where

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:52.250
the user should be directed to.

01:14:52.250 --> 01:14:54.860
So we have to do all that
under 100 milliseconds

01:14:54.860 --> 01:14:59.720
for each request, and we receive
about over 600,000 bit requests

01:14:59.720 --> 01:15:03.440
per second, at peak, and we
receive over 40 billion bit

01:15:03.440 --> 01:15:05.040
requests per day.

01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:08.660
So that is one of the challenges
we faced, and latency is

01:15:08.660 --> 01:15:10.770
a big problem we have to solve.

01:15:10.770 --> 01:15:12.200
So for that, we use Bigtable.

01:15:12.200 --> 01:15:14.570
I'll talk a little bit
more about this later.

01:15:14.570 --> 01:15:16.670
Transforming big data into
machine learning models

01:15:16.670 --> 01:15:17.840
is another challenge.

01:15:17.840 --> 01:15:21.200
We use a lot of complex
machine learning models.

01:15:21.200 --> 01:15:23.600
And to do that, we have
to be able to process

01:15:23.600 --> 01:15:25.070
a large volume of
data, and we have

01:15:25.070 --> 01:15:27.410
to transform that
data into something

01:15:27.410 --> 01:15:29.390
that our machine
learning algorithms

01:15:29.390 --> 01:15:31.470
can process and digest.

01:15:31.470 --> 01:15:33.530
So we use DataFlow for that.

01:15:33.530 --> 01:15:36.860
And we process over 100
terabytes of new data

01:15:36.860 --> 01:15:38.110
on a daily basis.

01:15:38.110 --> 01:15:39.830
And we also have
the accumulated data

01:15:39.830 --> 01:15:42.430
over the course of
two to three years

01:15:42.430 --> 01:15:46.430
that we use to train our
machine learning models.

01:15:46.430 --> 01:15:49.160
The third challenge was to
find a collaborative tool that

01:15:49.160 --> 01:15:51.870
can handle big data as
well as complex analysis.

01:15:51.870 --> 01:15:53.570
So for that, we use BigQuery.

01:15:53.570 --> 01:15:58.190
As you saw from the
demos by Win and Reza,

01:15:58.190 --> 01:16:01.550
we use BigQuery to
save views or queries

01:16:01.550 --> 01:16:04.640
and share those with
the team members.

01:16:04.640 --> 01:16:06.290
Our data scientists
and modelers want

01:16:06.290 --> 01:16:09.650
to analyze big data using
complex queries, SQL,

01:16:09.650 --> 01:16:12.380
or user-defined functions,
and also sometimes

01:16:12.380 --> 01:16:17.310
its very helpful to share
the queries themselves.

01:16:17.310 --> 01:16:19.610
So with that in mind,
I'm going to give you

01:16:19.610 --> 01:16:22.970
a high-level overview of how we
are using each of these Google

01:16:22.970 --> 01:16:27.120
Cloud platform products
to build our application.

01:16:27.120 --> 01:16:29.000
So on the left-hand
side, you see two boxes

01:16:29.000 --> 01:16:30.770
that are labeled as
real-time bidding

01:16:30.770 --> 01:16:33.620
exchanges and mobile
measurement platforms.

01:16:33.620 --> 01:16:36.260
So those are the
third-party services

01:16:36.260 --> 01:16:38.000
that we are integrated with.

01:16:38.000 --> 01:16:41.150
There are dozens of them, and we
receive live traffic from them.

01:16:41.150 --> 01:16:44.600
So as soon as we receive a bit
request or some other events

01:16:44.600 --> 01:16:47.540
from these services,
Google's load balancer

01:16:47.540 --> 01:16:50.990
will handle them and re-route
them to our live bid servers.

01:16:50.990 --> 01:16:53.120
Our live bid servers
are written in Golang,

01:16:53.120 --> 01:16:57.050
and they are running on
top of Kubernetes engine.

01:16:57.050 --> 01:17:00.410
So those two red boxes
represent our serving module.

01:17:00.410 --> 01:17:03.500
There are some other
components, like Pub/Sub,

01:17:03.500 --> 01:17:06.110
but it's not in this
diagram, because we

01:17:06.110 --> 01:17:08.690
are going to focus on data
processing in this session.

01:17:08.690 --> 01:17:12.570
So for big data processing, we
primarily use four products--

01:17:12.570 --> 01:17:15.830
DataFlow along with BigTable,
BigQuery, and Google Cloud

01:17:15.830 --> 01:17:16.374
Storage.

01:17:16.374 --> 01:17:18.290
So I'm going to talk
more about these products

01:17:18.290 --> 01:17:19.760
and how we use them.

01:17:19.760 --> 01:17:24.390
For modeling, especially
handling large datasets,

01:17:24.390 --> 01:17:26.780
TensorFlow is an open source
library released by Google,

01:17:26.780 --> 01:17:29.300
and most of our
machine learning models

01:17:29.300 --> 01:17:33.790
are written using TensorFlow.

01:17:33.790 --> 01:17:36.130
So if we zoom into data
processing, as I mentioned,

01:17:36.130 --> 01:17:39.280
we process over 100 terabytes
of data on a daily basis.

01:17:39.280 --> 01:17:41.530
So DataFlow jobs can
read from and write

01:17:41.530 --> 01:17:43.330
to BigTable,
BigQuery, Google Cloud

01:17:43.330 --> 01:17:46.900
Storage, including Pub/Sub, as
you saw from the demo earlier.

01:17:46.900 --> 01:17:52.240
So we use DataFlow to primarily
transform one complex data

01:17:52.240 --> 01:17:53.480
structure into another.

01:17:53.480 --> 01:17:56.650
So we keep transforming
data depending on our needs,

01:17:56.650 --> 01:17:58.930
and then eventually
our goal is to make

01:17:58.930 --> 01:18:01.690
it available at the time of
bidding so that we can make

01:18:01.690 --> 01:18:04.180
an informed decision about
how we want to participate

01:18:04.180 --> 01:18:07.950
in a real-time auction, and also
to train our machine learning

01:18:07.950 --> 01:18:08.740
models.

01:18:08.740 --> 01:18:14.200
So on a daily basis, we run
over 250 DataFlow batch jobs,

01:18:14.200 --> 01:18:18.790
and some of the largest jobs
use over 4,000 virtual CPUs

01:18:18.790 --> 01:18:19.550
per job.

01:18:19.550 --> 01:18:22.840
And most of our jobs are CPU
intensive because they're

01:18:22.840 --> 01:18:27.590
transforming a very complex
data structure to another.

01:18:27.590 --> 01:18:30.280
We also utilize DataFlow's
service-based shuffle.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:32.660
It's an opt-in feature
that you can use.

01:18:32.660 --> 01:18:35.290
I'm going to talk about
this a little later.

01:18:35.290 --> 01:18:38.260
And it actually gave us
huge performance gains

01:18:38.260 --> 01:18:42.650
in terms of throughput per cost.

01:18:42.650 --> 01:18:44.600
Before I talk about
some of the benefits

01:18:44.600 --> 01:18:47.660
we got by using
Google Cloud Platform,

01:18:47.660 --> 01:18:49.370
I also want to
emphasize how we were

01:18:49.370 --> 01:18:52.520
able to scale while keeping
the costs low in the past two

01:18:52.520 --> 01:18:53.180
years.

01:18:53.180 --> 01:18:57.510
So we have grown from
serving 60,000 QPS to 600,000

01:18:57.510 --> 01:18:58.960
QPS in the past two years.

01:18:58.960 --> 01:19:02.360
So this is 10 times of
growth in the number of bids.

01:19:02.360 --> 01:19:05.060
So here in the chart,
the x-axis is the month,

01:19:05.060 --> 01:19:06.830
and the y-axis on
the left-hand side

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:08.990
is the number of
bids or auctions

01:19:08.990 --> 01:19:11.750
we participated in
in a given month.

01:19:11.750 --> 01:19:13.290
And the blue bars
represent that.

01:19:13.290 --> 01:19:16.770
So you can clearly see a
fast growing trend here.

01:19:16.770 --> 01:19:18.950
The red line represents
the unit cost--

01:19:18.950 --> 01:19:21.470
our unit infra cost per bid.

01:19:21.470 --> 01:19:24.860
So it's been going down
by a factor of three

01:19:24.860 --> 01:19:25.970
the past two years.

01:19:25.970 --> 01:19:30.380
So we have been able to do
that, even though our developer

01:19:30.380 --> 01:19:31.800
team was pretty small.

01:19:31.800 --> 01:19:33.890
We started with two
full-time engineers,

01:19:33.890 --> 01:19:36.440
and now we're about 10
full-time engineers.

01:19:36.440 --> 01:19:39.680
The whole company has
grown from about, I think,

01:19:39.680 --> 01:19:43.220
seven people to about 45 people
now in the past two years.

01:19:43.220 --> 01:19:46.880
So we were able to do that
because our IT operations

01:19:46.880 --> 01:19:48.000
burden was very low.

01:19:48.000 --> 01:19:52.220
So most of the products that
we use on Google Cloud Platform

01:19:52.220 --> 01:19:55.477
are handled by Google Cloud
engineers and Google Cloud

01:19:55.477 --> 01:19:56.060
support teams.

01:19:56.060 --> 01:19:59.150
So we don't have to worry
about operating those tools--

01:19:59.150 --> 01:20:02.390
like, for example, Bigtable,
BigQuery, and DataFlow.

01:20:02.390 --> 01:20:05.900
We just have to worry about
improving our applications

01:20:05.900 --> 01:20:07.220
and cloud solutions.

01:20:07.220 --> 01:20:10.460
So we were able to improve
our tools, build new features,

01:20:10.460 --> 01:20:12.680
and add more tools,
but we didn't really

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:16.820
have to focus human resources
on this operations aspect.

01:20:16.820 --> 01:20:21.830
So that way we were able to
grow really fast, scale up

01:20:21.830 --> 01:20:25.160
really fast, while keeping
the unit costs very low,

01:20:25.160 --> 01:20:28.190
and here, the unit cost
in terms of infra bill

01:20:28.190 --> 01:20:31.960
as well as human resources.

01:20:31.960 --> 01:20:38.650
So in summary, I can list these
four benefits of using GCP.

01:20:38.650 --> 01:20:40.910
And then, in the
next two slides,

01:20:40.910 --> 01:20:43.930
I'm going to talk a little
more about specific use case

01:20:43.930 --> 01:20:44.610
studies--

01:20:44.610 --> 01:20:47.140
how we benefited from using GCP.

01:20:47.140 --> 01:20:50.290
So the first thing that comes
to my mind is a pay-as-you-go

01:20:50.290 --> 01:20:51.130
pricing model.

01:20:51.130 --> 01:20:54.100
So when we were a
very small company,

01:20:54.100 --> 01:20:58.810
we were not able to commit to
a huge amount of resources,

01:20:58.810 --> 01:21:01.180
like CPUs, or memory,
or virtual machines,

01:21:01.180 --> 01:21:04.250
because we were not
receiving that much traffic.

01:21:04.250 --> 01:21:07.510
But now, if you are building
a scalable application,

01:21:07.510 --> 01:21:10.090
keeping the unit costs
the same or lower

01:21:10.090 --> 01:21:12.280
will be very
beneficial as you grow,

01:21:12.280 --> 01:21:15.325
because overall, your
application's scalability

01:21:15.325 --> 01:21:19.540
is going up as long as
your unit price is at level

01:21:19.540 --> 01:21:20.260
or going down.

01:21:20.260 --> 01:21:22.100
So that's exactly what we did.

01:21:22.100 --> 01:21:24.360
And now that we have
grown quite a bit,

01:21:24.360 --> 01:21:27.220
we are also utilizing what
is called the Committed Use

01:21:27.220 --> 01:21:27.860
Discount.

01:21:27.860 --> 01:21:32.550
So we have committed to use
a certain amount of vCPUs

01:21:32.550 --> 01:21:36.280
and virtual machines in
each of the major regions

01:21:36.280 --> 01:21:38.260
that Google Cloud
Platform provides,

01:21:38.260 --> 01:21:41.320
so we are able to cut down
our infra bill as well.

01:21:41.320 --> 01:21:43.420
Another aspect is--
this was mentioned

01:21:43.420 --> 01:21:45.670
earlier in the session,
too-- but easy integration

01:21:45.670 --> 01:21:47.470
among many products of GCP.

01:21:47.470 --> 01:21:50.050
In particular, in my
personal experience,

01:21:50.050 --> 01:21:53.530
using DataFlow together with
Bigtable, BigQuery, and Google

01:21:53.530 --> 01:21:55.360
Cloud Storage has
been very useful,

01:21:55.360 --> 01:22:01.690
because they provide a lot of
useful APIs and documentation.

01:22:01.690 --> 01:22:03.490
So operationally,
we didn't really

01:22:03.490 --> 01:22:07.570
need a separate
operations team that is

01:22:07.570 --> 01:22:09.250
separate from developers team.

01:22:09.250 --> 01:22:11.650
So we didn't really
need to worry

01:22:11.650 --> 01:22:15.100
about how we're going to manage
or operate or deploy some

01:22:15.100 --> 01:22:16.990
of the services
offered by Google Cloud

01:22:16.990 --> 01:22:20.440
Platform, because they
are all handled by Google.

01:22:20.440 --> 01:22:24.160
So that has been
very useful for us.

01:22:24.160 --> 01:22:26.170
Stack Overflow is
a great resource

01:22:26.170 --> 01:22:29.440
for asking technical questions
on some of these products.

01:22:29.440 --> 01:22:33.430
Personally, I've asked a bunch
of questions about DataFlow

01:22:33.430 --> 01:22:37.220
and Bigtable on Stack Overflow,
and the team members--

01:22:37.220 --> 01:22:38.650
team engineers at Google--

01:22:38.650 --> 01:22:41.350
actually responded to
some of my questions.

01:22:41.350 --> 01:22:43.250
And they were very helpful.

01:22:43.250 --> 01:22:47.200
So that's one of the great
resources-- public resources--

01:22:47.200 --> 01:22:48.370
you can utilize.

01:22:48.370 --> 01:22:50.140
In the next two
slides, I'm going

01:22:50.140 --> 01:22:54.340
to talk more specifically
about our case studies of how

01:22:54.340 --> 01:22:57.820
we utilize DataFlow's
service-based shuffle, which

01:22:57.820 --> 01:22:59.710
is an opt-in feature,
and also, how

01:22:59.710 --> 01:23:02.410
we use the BigTable and
BigQuery as complements

01:23:02.410 --> 01:23:07.750
to overcome the three major
challenges I mentioned earlier.

01:23:07.750 --> 01:23:10.390
So Dataflow
service-based shuffle

01:23:10.390 --> 01:23:14.100
is basically an opt-in
feature that DataFlow back end

01:23:14.100 --> 01:23:15.070
server provides.

01:23:15.070 --> 01:23:17.860
So you pay based on
the amount of data

01:23:17.860 --> 01:23:19.720
processed by the server.

01:23:19.720 --> 01:23:23.080
But in exchange,
your job's throughput

01:23:23.080 --> 01:23:26.470
and performances in
terms of just duration

01:23:26.470 --> 01:23:28.310
are expected to get better.

01:23:28.310 --> 01:23:33.250
So in this chart, the top
chart shows the same job CPU

01:23:33.250 --> 01:23:36.070
utilization without
the shuffle service,

01:23:36.070 --> 01:23:38.770
and the bottom chart shows
it with the shuffle service.

01:23:38.770 --> 01:23:41.020
So the images are
of the same size,

01:23:41.020 --> 01:23:43.100
but the first job
took about four hours,

01:23:43.100 --> 01:23:45.340
and the second job
took about three hours.

01:23:45.340 --> 01:23:48.070
And y-axis is the
CPU utilization.

01:23:48.070 --> 01:23:50.590
As I mentioned earlier,
most of our jobs

01:23:50.590 --> 01:23:51.820
are very CPU intensive.

01:23:51.820 --> 01:23:55.150
So if CPU utilization
is low, that

01:23:55.150 --> 01:23:57.760
means our job's
throughput is not at peak.

01:23:57.760 --> 01:24:01.390
So clearly, you can see how it
is better with the second job

01:24:01.390 --> 01:24:03.310
when we turned on
the shuffle service.

01:24:03.310 --> 01:24:06.550
And overall, you can also save--

01:24:06.550 --> 01:24:08.260
because your job
duration is shorter,

01:24:08.260 --> 01:24:10.510
you will pay less
for the resources

01:24:10.510 --> 01:24:13.520
you used for running
those DataFlow jobs.

01:24:13.520 --> 01:24:17.980
But you can also save
your costs by cutting down

01:24:17.980 --> 01:24:21.810
memory or disk space used
by these DataFlow jobs.

01:24:21.810 --> 01:24:25.930
So overall, in this specific
case, it gave us a three times

01:24:25.930 --> 01:24:28.420
improvement in
throughput per cost,

01:24:28.420 --> 01:24:31.870
and we used this shuffle service
for some of our batch jobs

01:24:31.870 --> 01:24:35.770
whenever it is applicable.

01:24:35.770 --> 01:24:38.110
Another thing I
want to emphasize

01:24:38.110 --> 01:24:41.020
is how we use Bigtable,
BigQuery DataFlow together as

01:24:41.020 --> 01:24:41.920
complements.

01:24:41.920 --> 01:24:44.920
So, as I mentioned earlier,
one of the challenges

01:24:44.920 --> 01:24:47.500
we have is latency requirement.

01:24:47.500 --> 01:24:49.750
So Bigtable for quick
access is basically

01:24:49.750 --> 01:24:52.050
how we have overcome
the first challenge.

01:24:52.050 --> 01:24:54.460
Bigtable offers quick
key-value operations,

01:24:54.460 --> 01:24:58.090
both read and write, at 10,000
QPS under six millisecond.

01:24:58.090 --> 01:25:00.550
And that's actually very
quick, and we really

01:25:00.550 --> 01:25:03.880
need this to meet the 100
millisecond requirement.

01:25:03.880 --> 01:25:06.880
And it also offers fast
scans using DataFlow--

01:25:06.880 --> 01:25:08.840
so 200 megabytes per second--

01:25:08.840 --> 01:25:10.120
and this is per node.

01:25:10.120 --> 01:25:13.240
So we store a lot
of data in BigTable

01:25:13.240 --> 01:25:18.430
that is required for determining
whether we want to participate

01:25:18.430 --> 01:25:19.920
in a real-time bidding or not.

01:25:19.920 --> 01:25:21.670
And also, for our
machine learning models,

01:25:21.670 --> 01:25:23.740
they have to determine
what bid price

01:25:23.740 --> 01:25:28.440
we are going to write and
submit to those exchanges.

01:25:28.440 --> 01:25:31.150
So we have a lot of
data stored in Bigtable,

01:25:31.150 --> 01:25:36.190
and DataFlow is one way to
scan through this Bigtable

01:25:36.190 --> 01:25:41.590
and store the resulting dataset
in BigQuery or Google Cloud

01:25:41.590 --> 01:25:42.250
Storage.

01:25:42.250 --> 01:25:46.150
Also, garbage collection is
very easy with different types

01:25:46.150 --> 01:25:47.750
of garbage collection policies.

01:25:47.750 --> 01:25:51.730
So that's another useful
feature for Bigtable.

01:25:51.730 --> 01:25:54.980
And BigQuery is more suitable
for complex analysis.

01:25:54.980 --> 01:25:56.820
So our data scientists
and modelers

01:25:56.820 --> 01:26:00.340
would use BigQuery to analyze
complex data, including

01:26:00.340 --> 01:26:03.550
our training datasets for
machine learning models.

01:26:03.550 --> 01:26:06.340
So SQL and user-defined
functions are supported.

01:26:06.340 --> 01:26:11.530
And also, we use stream inserts
for real-time monitoring

01:26:11.530 --> 01:26:12.610
and analytics.

01:26:12.610 --> 01:26:16.160
So our live bid
servers will stream

01:26:16.160 --> 01:26:17.770
insert some of the data--

01:26:17.770 --> 01:26:19.330
like logs, and some metrics--

01:26:19.330 --> 01:26:20.620
to BigQuery tables.

01:26:20.620 --> 01:26:22.240
And also, as you
saw from the demo,

01:26:22.240 --> 01:26:24.170
it is very easy to
share queries and views.

01:26:24.170 --> 01:26:27.640
So this basically solved
our third challenge,

01:26:27.640 --> 01:26:31.150
where we needed a collaborative
tool that can handle big data,

01:26:31.150 --> 01:26:34.480
and it's very easy to share
within the organization.

01:26:34.480 --> 01:26:37.210
And also, as you
saw from the demo,

01:26:37.210 --> 01:26:40.560
garbage collection policy or
table expiration can be set,

01:26:40.560 --> 01:26:43.390
so that's another plus.

01:26:43.390 --> 01:26:45.760
To utilize both
of these products,

01:26:45.760 --> 01:26:48.730
we use DataFlow as
the main product

01:26:48.730 --> 01:26:50.830
that does data transformation.

01:26:50.830 --> 01:26:53.830
So specifically for our
machine learning pipeline,

01:26:53.830 --> 01:26:56.950
we have to generate a large
collection of datasets

01:26:56.950 --> 01:26:59.560
to train our models
and evaluate them.

01:26:59.560 --> 01:27:03.300
So most valuable
information or dataset

01:27:03.300 --> 01:27:06.760
is stored in Bigtable, because
they are read and written

01:27:06.760 --> 01:27:08.740
by our live bid servers.

01:27:08.740 --> 01:27:12.550
So we use DataFlow job to
scan the whole Bigtable

01:27:12.550 --> 01:27:15.130
and dump that data to
Google Cloud Storage as well

01:27:15.130 --> 01:27:16.240
as BigQuery.

01:27:16.240 --> 01:27:19.390
The reason we write the
data to two different places

01:27:19.390 --> 01:27:22.810
is because Google Cloud Storage
is basically just a storage.

01:27:22.810 --> 01:27:25.550
We store all of our data there.

01:27:25.550 --> 01:27:28.430
On the other hand, BigQuery is
suitable for complex analysis.

01:27:28.430 --> 01:27:30.310
So we are going to
transform this data--

01:27:30.310 --> 01:27:32.770
the same data that we
read from Bigtable--

01:27:32.770 --> 01:27:34.690
in a way that our
modelers and data

01:27:34.690 --> 01:27:39.850
scientists can easily analyze
on BigQuery using SQL.

01:27:39.850 --> 01:27:44.290
So that's how we generate
machine learning training

01:27:44.290 --> 01:27:49.390
datasets, and then
our TensorFlow models

01:27:49.390 --> 01:27:51.240
will train from those datasets.

01:27:54.180 --> 01:27:57.630
So just to wrap up,
Moloco has grown rapidly

01:27:57.630 --> 01:27:59.940
in the past two
years, and scalability

01:27:59.940 --> 01:28:02.320
has been the key to our growth.

01:28:02.320 --> 01:28:05.430
Google Cloud Platform helped
us to focus more on developing

01:28:05.430 --> 01:28:08.580
our application--
ad cloud solution--

01:28:08.580 --> 01:28:11.700
without having to worry about
operating some of these cloud

01:28:11.700 --> 01:28:14.610
services that you saw
from this session.

01:28:14.610 --> 01:28:18.810
So in particular, by using
Bigtable, BigQuery, DataFlow,

01:28:18.810 --> 01:28:21.720
we were able to overcome
those three major challenges I

01:28:21.720 --> 01:28:24.420
mentioned in the first
slide, and that's

01:28:24.420 --> 01:28:27.510
how we have been able to
work with some of the largest

01:28:27.510 --> 01:28:29.580
clients we have, like
largest e-commerce

01:28:29.580 --> 01:28:33.090
in Asia, or one of the largest
electronic device makers.

01:28:33.090 --> 01:28:36.720
So with that, I'm going to
give this back to William.

01:28:36.720 --> 01:28:39.442
And, yeah, thank you.

01:28:39.442 --> 01:28:44.060
WILLIAM VAMBENEPE: Thank
you very much, Hooyeon.

01:28:44.060 --> 01:28:44.770
Thank you.

01:28:44.770 --> 01:28:48.640
And so, just in closing, you
see those three principles

01:28:48.640 --> 01:28:51.220
which I described in an
abstract way at the beginning.

01:28:51.220 --> 01:28:54.330
Hopefully, by now, you
see how, in practice,

01:28:54.330 --> 01:28:58.010
the data integration delivers
on the integrated part,

01:28:58.010 --> 01:29:00.190
or Composer tying
things together,

01:29:00.190 --> 01:29:05.590
how Pub/Sub and DataFlow and
BigQuery really exemplify

01:29:05.590 --> 01:29:08.260
serverless for data
processing, and how,

01:29:08.260 --> 01:29:11.830
whether it's bringing the best
of open-source to Google with

01:29:11.830 --> 01:29:15.010
DataProc, with Composer,
or vice versa--

01:29:15.010 --> 01:29:18.690
bringing Google technology to
open-source with TensorFlow,

01:29:18.690 --> 01:29:20.770
and Apache Beam, and others--

01:29:20.770 --> 01:29:24.310
we live by this
principle of everything

01:29:24.310 --> 01:29:26.290
we do being based
on open technology.

01:29:26.290 --> 01:29:29.260
And so, hopefully, that
gave you a quick overview

01:29:29.260 --> 01:29:32.260
of how we apply those
principles, a quick overview

01:29:32.260 --> 01:29:34.780
of the capabilities of
some of the key services.

01:29:34.780 --> 01:29:36.880
